# The shape no other benchmark has: one accumulator per iteration of an outer
# loop, each grown inside an inner loop and none kept. deepbench carries a
# short accumulator ACROSS a bind and widebench carries one long list for the
# whole run; both allocate a handful of buffers outside the arena and hold them
# to exit, which is bounded by the program text. Here the count is bounded by
# the input, which is what a simulator does — vse builds an electorate per
# trial and drops it.
#
# It exists because the fix that stopped this leaking priced as pure cost:
# every benchmark in the corpus paid for the check and none could collect,
# because none leaked. A corpus that cannot represent a failure mode licenses
# trading it away.
#
# The accumulator is seeded ONE FRAME OUT and that is the whole trick. A header
# born inside the inner loop dies with its rewind and never escapes; a header
# that predates the beat IS the loop's accumulator, and its storage has to go
# where a rewind cannot reach.
#
# The element pushed is arithmetic rather than a call, and that is not a
# stylistic choice: a call in this position removes the beat bracket
# altogether, so the loop stops sweeping and this benchmark measures nothing.
# See task #225.
span = 400

fn filled acc _ 0
  acc

fn filled acc k n
  filled (push acc ((k * 31 + n * 7 + 3) % 997)) k (n - 1)

fn one_list k
  base = push [] 1
  length (filled base k span)

fn many at stop tally
  more at stop tally (at > stop)

fn more _ _ tally true
  tally

fn more at stop tally false
  many (at + 1) stop (tally + one_list at)

pub fn total n
  many 1 n 0
